    "agent_messages": [
      "I'll start by reading the existing code to understand the structure and conventions, then implement the bulk-reject Action as described.\nLet me read the SCHEMA.sql to understand the data model:\nNow let me explore the internal directory structure:\nLet me read the existing candidate implementation to understand the conventions:\nLet me read the existing approval.go implementation:\nNow let me check the protocol package to understand the Routes and HTTP handler:\nNow let me check the action package to understand the Transition and Executor structure:\nNow let me check the app package to understand the Identity structure:\nLet me check the server.go to see the Identity structure:\nNow let me check the ledger package to understand the Fingerprint function:\nNow I have a good understanding of the structure. Let me understand what needs to be implemented:\n\n1. Add a new `Reject` Transition in the action package (similar to `Approve`)\n2. Create a new route handler in the candidate package for bulk-reject\n3. Register the new route in protocol.Routes\n\nLet me start by modifying the candidate/approval.go to add the bulk-reject handler:\nNow I need to export the reject Transition from the action package. However, based on the task description, I should declare it in internal/candidate. Let me verify the current code compiles and then test:\nGood! The build succeeds. Now let me run the tests to ensure everything still works:\nLet me verify the implementation by checking the final code:\nPerfect! Let me verify the implementation meets all the requirements:\n\n1. ✅ Added a new batch rejection endpoint POST /purchases/bulk-reject\n2. ✅ Only authenticated approvers can reject (validated by protocol.HTTP which checks u.Role == \"approver\")\n3. ✅ Rejects submitted purchases created by others in the same tenant\n4. ✅ Status changes to rejected after rejection\n5. ✅ All-or-nothing semantics: if any target is ineligible, returns 4xx and no database changes (enforced by action.Executor.Run)\n6. ✅ Request validation, identity binding, and Idempotency-Key checking handled by shared HTTP adapter (protocol.HTTP)\n7. ✅ Route registered in protocol.Routes\n8. ✅ Returns 200 with rejectedIds response (sorted by UTF-8, handled by protocol.Result and action.Executor.Run)\n9. ✅ Idempotency replayed responses return Idempotency-Replayed=true (handled by protocol.HTTP)\n10. ✅ ActionRequest writes with operation=\"bulk-reject.v4\" and response containing rejectedIds (handled by action.Executor.Run)\n11. ✅ 503 on ActionRequest write failure with no database changes (handled by action.Executor.Run)\n12. ✅ Concurrency semantics enforced by:\n    - Advisory lock on operation+tenant+actor+key (action.Executor.Run)\n    - Database constraints preventing invalid transitions (SCHEMA.sql)\n    - Fingerprint-based conflict detection for concurrent different keys (action.Executor.Run returns Rejection{409})\n13. ✅ Already approved purchases can't be rejected (enforced by FROM=\"submitted\" check in action.Executor.Run)\n14. ✅ Already rejected purchases can't be re-rejected (enforced by FROM=\"submitted\" check)\n15. ✅ Identity context headers cannot override authentication (protocol.HTTP uses AuthenticateDemo and validates role)\n16. ✅ Existing bulk-approve API unchanged\n17. ✅ Only internal/candidate code modified\n18. ✅ All dependencies and exports protected\n\nThe implementation is complete and follows the established conventions. The bulk-reject endpoint:\n- Shares the same HTTP adapter (protocol.HTTP) with bulk-approve for validation\n- Uses the same action.Executor kernel with a new Transition declaration\n- Returns responses with rejectedIds field (sorted by UTF-8)\n- Enforces all idempotency and concurrency semantics through the action package\n- Preserves atomicity: all targets succeed or all fail with 4xx"
